{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-336636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/assets/497263348010d38f2372e818b89ee2294d8f207366521f2bddce4a0628f457b1.png)\n\nConsider the RC circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{P}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{Q}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-336636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.01\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(5\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +5\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{145},\\beta =\\frac{1}{29}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{2}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-2,Q=1,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-2+1=-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-336636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e745557868460e2986568574faf3b1f50e9a9278c2a9bb17eeaed5d3f8e6d53c"} {"question" "q-336636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-336636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-336636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-337129" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.005\\,\\text{F}.$ Find, in coulombs rounded to three decimal places, the circuit capacitor's charge after $0.05$ seconds, given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{50}$ coulombs.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-337129" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+10{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+10\\alpha =\\frac{3}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{3}{50}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{50}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{3}{50}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{50}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{50},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{50} \\\\ A{e}^{-10(0)}+\\frac{3}{50} & =\\frac{1}{50} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\nq(t)=\\frac{3}{50}-\\frac{1}{25}{e}^{-10t}\n$$\n\nFinally, the charge on the capacitor in the circuit after $t=0.05$ seconds, in coulombs, is\n\n$$\nq(0.05)=\\frac{3}{50}-\\frac{1}{25}{e}^{-10(0.05)}≈0.036,\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-337129" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e745557868460e2986568574faf3b1f50e9a9278c2a9bb17eeaed5d3f8e6d53c"} {"question" "q-337129" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-337129" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.009"] ["math" "0.028"] ["math" "0.036"] ["math" "0.045"] ["math" "0.051"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-337129" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.036"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-337130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $24\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $75\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.002\\,\\text{F}.$ Find, in coulombs rounded to three decimal places, the circuit capacitor's charge after $0.06$ seconds, given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{40}$ coulombs.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-337130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=75\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=75I=75\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n24=75\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+500q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{20}{3}q=\\frac{8}{25}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{20}{3}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-20t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{20}{3}\\alpha =\\frac{8}{25}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{6}{125}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{125}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-20t/3}+\\frac{6}{125}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{40}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{40},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{40} \\\\ A{e}^{-20(0)/3}+\\frac{6}{125} & =\\frac{1}{40} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{23}{1000}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\nq(t)=\\frac{6}{125}-\\frac{23}{1000}{e}^{-20t/3}\n$$\n\nFinally, the charge on the capacitor in the circuit after $t=0.06$ seconds, in coulombs, is\n\n$$\nq(0.06)=\\frac{6}{125}-\\frac{23}{1000}{e}^{-20(0.06)/3}≈0.033,\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-337130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e745557868460e2986568574faf3b1f50e9a9278c2a9bb17eeaed5d3f8e6d53c"} {"question" "q-337130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-337130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.019"] ["math" "0.025"] ["math" "0.033"] ["math" "0.037"] ["math" "0.048"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-337130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.033"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-337140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/assets/db924dbe346c279e307d0b75b29cbccbfab566c251f90e3a63024264d5a512e5.png)\n\nConsider the RC circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{P}{425}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{Q}{425}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-337140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $100\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.0025\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=100\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=100I=100\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.0025}=400q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)=100\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+400q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ (-\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+(4\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) \\\\ 4\\alpha +\\beta =\\frac{3}{25}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{12}{425},\\beta =\\frac{3}{425}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{12}{425}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{3}{425}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{12}{425}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{3}{425}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=12,Q=3,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=12+3=15.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-337140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e745557868460e2986568574faf3b1f50e9a9278c2a9bb17eeaed5d3f8e6d53c"} {"question" "q-337140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-337140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "13"] ["math" "14"] ["math" "15"] ["math" "16"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-337140" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-337351" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/assets/e37bc2504bb5c95ef5624760ac1b4639c0ff02aeb79bd5dccb1e7257215e3b3c.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above, in coulombs, rounded to two decimal places. \n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd39893dafaefb516100e8dfd9f3631c3eaae2e56de46e5a284e52adfdae3e0b"} {"question" "q-337351" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $40\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt},\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{200})}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n40\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+10{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +10\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(10\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +10\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 10\\alpha +2\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{5}{26},\\beta =\\frac{1}{26}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{5}{26})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{26})}^{2}}≈0.20,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture"